[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generics, Wildcards & Type Erasure in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering generics, wildcards, and type erasure is crucial for writing type-safe, reusable Android components. Whether you are building custom View wrappers, Repository patterns, or working with collections, understanding these concepts prevents runtime ClassCastExceptions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Basic Generics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics allow you to abstract types, enabling classes, interfaces, and methods to operate on objects of various types while providing compile-time type safety. In Android, this is frequently seen in architectural components like LiveData or Resource wrappers."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Resource<T> {
    public final T data;
    public final String message;

    public Resource(T data, String message) {
        this.data = data;
        this.message = message;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Bounded Type Parameters"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you want to restrict the types that can be passed as type arguments. In Android, you might want a generic method that only accepts subclasses of View. You achieve this using bounded type parameters with the extends keyword."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public <T extends View> void hideView(T view) {
    view.setVisibility(View.GONE);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Wildcards (?): Upper and Lower Bounds"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Wildcards are represented by the question mark (?) and are used as type parameters. They provide flexibility when passing parameters to methods. The PECS rule (Producer Extends, Consumer Super) is essential here."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Upper Bounded Wildcards (? extends T): Use when your method reads from a collection. The collection acts as a producer."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lower Bounded Wildcards (? super T): Use when your method writes to a collection. The collection acts as a consumer."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public void processNumberList(List<? extends Number> numbers) {
    for (Number n : numbers) {
        Log.d("Generics", n.toString());
    }
}

public void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> list) {
    list.add(42);
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Type Erasure in Java"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java implements generics via type erasure. This means the compiler removes all generic type parameters and replaces them with their bounds (or Object if unbounded) during compilation. Consequently, generic type information is not available at runtime."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because of type erasure, expressions like `if (item instanceof List<String>)` will fail to compile. You can only check against the raw type: `if (item instanceof List)`."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Working Around Type Erasure in Android"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Android development, libraries like Gson or Room often need runtime generic type information (e.g., parsing JSON into List<User>). To bypass type erasure, developers use TypeToken patterns."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"Type userListType = new TypeToken<List<User>>() {}.getType();
List<User> users = gson.fromJson(jsonString, userListType);"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By using an anonymous subclass, the generic type information is captured in the class file's signature, making it accessible via reflection at runtime despite type erasure."}]
